    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-14981",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/06b772f72fe6d6f1ee02bdc961f6438561083573e0e0d9d261c7594cd48e86bc.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that there exists \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/c4ebc4da7c8037d0ca6a752df8a4dc3933918db39ac972e4a9b5068fac834140.svg) such that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/88e94f9f45ba18035aaea40fb665ecd374c878f9ac7c44aeb038f2d210c02a28.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/5aaf3d46d0bf568ead42806495080fa6eab9d567aa33d22faeee925233867b0a.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3 \\mid 18$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$ $k$ such that $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}18 = 3k}.$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/repairs/completeness-3160fa2c4e896719/source/390c1ec221abdfe72fb1acb5dc6a5b45748be6da639f972ac7d85786774aa9b9.html",
